  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "d5b66cea-231a-4e6d-b656-9bb406d80bd9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "’Intelligence’ is one of those words we all use confidently until someone asks us to define it. Today, we’re going to pin down and test our own definitions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5b66cea-231a-4e6d-b656-9bb406d80bd9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5eef647-5d88-4c29-b311-addce6ad8af0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When you call a person—or an animal, or a computer—”intelligent,” what do you really mean?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prime students’ thinking about what ‘intelligence’ means. Students should surface their working definitions and notice they’re not all the same. Strong responses describe what the brain (or a system) is doing, not just its outcomes: for example, “learns from mistakes,” or “figures things out through trial and error.” Watch for outcome-oriented answers, like “gets good grades.” When you hear these, push students towards the underlying mechanism: “What is the brain doing that makes getting good grades possible?” If students struggle to describe what makes things intelligent, ask them for examples of objects that are not intelligent and compare: what makes some things intelligent and others not? To end the discussion, acknowledge that it’s okay to not agree on a single definition of intelligence. The point is simply to start to think about what ‘intelligence’ really means."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5eef647-5d88-4c29-b311-addce6ad8af0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e1307fb6-6dcb-46fa-9836-bb37eae473c7",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "It’s okay for us not to have a clear definition of ‘intelligence’ yet. For the next few minutes, I want you to write down your own definition based on what you shared or heard from other classmates.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e1307fb6-6dcb-46fa-9836-bb37eae473c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b49d7f98-ccea-4cbc-bc34-6e70d67d4215",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Logic-Based AI Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students 1–3 minutes to write down their working definition of intelligence in the Lesson 1 Warm Up section. Tell them they’ll come back to it at the end of class to refine it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b49d7f98-ccea-4cbc-bc34-6e70d67d4215",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e1b145ef-f47c-47cf-ae2d-3e2297cd3aec"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b5ac0d5-7a79-4f99-aac2-7435a10e3b89",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You have a working definition of ‘intelligence.’ Now, let's stress-test it. I'm going to give each pair of you a deck of cards. Each card describes a behavior—sometimes from a person, sometimes from an animal, sometimes from a machine. Your job is to decide what kind of 'intelligence'—if any—that behavior represents, and to group cards that show the same kind of intelligence together. There's no single right answer. What matters is that your group can defend the way you sorted.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Intelligence Card Sort",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b5ac0d5-7a79-4f99-aac2-7435a10e3b89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f2bdecd6-f9c9-4e79-ac7c-6daedd8d6926",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Intelligence Card Sort instructions to students. Take questions before grouping.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students in pairs of two.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "It’s okay if, with an odd number of students, you have a group of three."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f2bdecd6-f9c9-4e79-ac7c-6daedd8d6926",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5aa1e59-0ccd-4c27-bc5d-b6d080efec62",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student an Intelligence Card Sort set.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Intelligence Card Sort instructions to students while they work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate**: Listen for groups that lump everything into one category (e.g., “It’s all just following rules”) or that split everything into “human” vs. “machine” without considering finer distinctions. If groups stall, ask: “Is the way a calculator solves a problem the same kind of thinking as the way a dog learns a new trick?” or “What about the thermostat—is that intelligence at all?” Reassure pairs who want a right answer that the goal is to surface their reasoning. Disagreements between groups are exactly what we want.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you decide which cards belonged together?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface students’ thinking from the Intelligence Card Sort activity. Students should recognize that intelligence isn’t one thing: it could be rule-following, learning from experience, responding to the environment, or reasoning about new situations. A strong response might name a category and describe why certain cards are part of it: for example, “these cards all learn from the experience, like the baby learning to walk.” Watch out for vague or outcome-only groupings: for example, “these are all the smart cards.” Offer more food for thought: “It’s good you think these cards are smart, but what are they doing that’s smart?” End the discussion by highlighting 2–3 different ways that pairs sorted their cards.”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5aa1e59-0ccd-4c27-bc5d-b6d080efec62",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a73546c-c49b-43d8-b8d0-000885c91d15",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you’ve just done—having different ideas about what counts as intelligence—is exactly the problem people have been arguing about for decades, especially when it comes to computers. In 1950, a mathematician named Alan Turing proposed one way to settle these debates. Let’s see what you think of his answer.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a73546c-c49b-43d8-b8d0-000885c91d15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "074b9278-4867-4dc4-b227-16b2c0ffdd48",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define the *Turing test*. Emphasize that it’s a behavioral test: it doesn’t care what’s happening inside the machine, only what comes out.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "The Turing Test in Action"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "074b9278-4867-4dc4-b227-16b2c0ffdd48",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88588601-71f2-480b-aa2d-78faa6738181",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Alan Turing was a mathematician who proposed a simple idea: to test for intelligence, it didn’t matter what a machine was actually doing—it only mattered whether it could hold a conversation as well as a human.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88588601-71f2-480b-aa2d-78faa6738181",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "af7600e3-7a03-48d4-ae2d-4017afaee5ce",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Turing’s goal was to make a reasonable assessment of artificial intelligence, or AI. Let’s define what we mean by AI before we move any further.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "af7600e3-7a03-48d4-ae2d-4017afaee5ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ec2a392d-f4df-40d7-8c2b-07a24d64c614",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *artificial intelligence*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ec2a392d-f4df-40d7-8c2b-07a24d64c614",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fab11ce4-e892-47d7-9261-1054f9754a86",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now, let’s see the Turing Test in action. In this test, a human interviewer talked to two musical artists—one of which was AI. Let’s see how the artists responded to three of the interviewer’s questions.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fab11ce4-e892-47d7-9261-1054f9754a86",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa294095-fce8-483c-89a7-3982ac7f60b3",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to raise their hand if they think A is the AI. Ask students to raise their hand if they think B is the AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa294095-fce8-483c-89a7-3982ac7f60b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58d924f4-8a04-42f3-9abf-457c82b33a0c",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s the next question and the two responses. Remember, one of these responses was generated by AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58d924f4-8a04-42f3-9abf-457c82b33a0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd76f544-623c-479c-a51f-cb47b37a90da",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to again raise their hand if they think A is the AI. Ask students to raise their hand if they think B is the AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd76f544-623c-479c-a51f-cb47b37a90da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "700c6fb6-02d2-48a5-bb35-9be2efb792ee",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Here’s the next question and the two responses. Remember, one of these responses was generated by AI.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "700c6fb6-02d2-48a5-bb35-9be2efb792ee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b578db7a-7448-4554-bf59-dea822dff7d8",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to once again raise their hand if they think A is the AI. Ask students to raise their hand if they think B is the AI."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b578db7a-7448-4554-bf59-dea822dff7d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5bbcf3ac-2395-4703-9274-57cd834f573a",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Are you ready for the reveal?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5bbcf3ac-2395-4703-9274-57cd834f573a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bbdcaa9-227d-4428-8b4a-84276153cecc",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Artist A was actually Taylor Swift in an interview for the New York Times. Artist B wasn’t real—instead, their responses were generated entirely by AI. If you thought Artist B was real, it passed the Turing test. So, what do you think? Is the Turing test an accurate test of intelligence? For the next 10 minutes, you’ll gather your thoughts and debate them with AI.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optionally introduce students to the comic at https://imgs.xkcd.com/comics/turing_test.png, which riffs on the fundamental idea behind the Turing test."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bbdcaa9-227d-4428-8b4a-84276153cecc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbafe8ab-8d67-4d22-9e30-9921dc3c5d55",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Debating Intelligence",
        "progression_name": "Debating Intelligence"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbafe8ab-8d67-4d22-9e30-9921dc3c5d55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6bea07c-c12d-4f19-983d-5bcb6dcef435",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Levels 1–3. Students stay on the platform through the rest of the activity.\n- Level 1: Students write down their stance on the Turing test as a measure of intelligence.\n- Level 2: Students debate their stance with AI, using it to strengthen or adjust their argument.\n- Level 3: Students revisit and refine their stance based on the debate. This serves as the lesson's formative assessment.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask students about their AI conversations on-screen. Look for:\n- Students who pick a side too quickly without engaging with counter arguments. You could ask: \"What's the best argument against your position? Has the AI tried it on you yet?\"\n- Students conflating \"fooling a human\" with \"thinking like a human.\" Ask students about whether these might be two different things..\n- Students who let the AI talk them out of their position without resistance. You could ask: \"Did the AI's argument actually mean something to you, or just sound persuasive?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 responses to gauge understanding before the Wrap Up.\n- If most students offer a clear stance with at least one substantive reason, move to the wrap-up.\n- If responses lean on surface reasoning (e.g., \"it's just a computer,\" \"it's smart because it's fast\"), surface 2–3 student responses anonymously and ask the class to push on them: \"What would the Turing test say about this argument?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6bea07c-c12d-4f19-983d-5bcb6dcef435",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d86be1b-c70c-4fe6-9323-bd5d865a9311",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Whether or not you ended up convinced by Turing, you've now done what computer scientists have argued about for 75 years: what should count as intelligence in a machine. There's no settled answer. But the answer you would give shapes what we mean when we say a machine is 'smart.' Hold onto your stance—we'll use it again.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d86be1b-c70c-4fe6-9323-bd5d865a9311",
        "lesson_activity.key": "21fac691-68b3-4d23-b5fb-5500025579f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7b7b3081-df64-41f3-9a00-54c277bcb4eb",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students back to their original definition of intelligence in the Unit Guide and have them refine it in light of today’s discussion. Give them about 90 seconds, then have 2–3 students share how their definition changed.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7b7b3081-df64-41f3-9a00-54c277bcb4eb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a6b703fc-1e0f-48ed-ac0e-6f2abd5c810e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce333c25-a39c-4414-8d6f-856761533292",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair students up with a partner.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students talk with their partner about one of the examples of AI on the slide. Students should discuss what each example is capable of and what they think makes each example work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As pairs discuss AI technologies with each other, listen for ways that students talk about intelligence.\n- If students describe a technology mostly in terms of output (e.g. “voice assistants answer questions”), consider prompting them to think about other intelligent parts of the process (e.g. How does the voice assistant know what words you said? How does it understand what your question means?)\n- To help students think about what inventions led to a technology, prompt students to think: “What does the computer have to figure out, step by step?” or “What had to exist before someone could build this?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What problems did people have to solve to create AI tools?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that AI is not a single idea or innovation, but involves solutions to many different problems. It’s okay if students don’t yet have a precise sense for those problems. If students struggle to identify problems, encourage them to brainstorm based on the different features of the AI tools: for example, a voice assistant needs to “listen to your voice” and “understand your question” and “speak an answer.”"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce333c25-a39c-4414-8d6f-856761533292",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e7f0096-f270-421a-bdba-088c9ca33cfe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "487cdf57-5012-4fe9-a475-0b0fb844b624",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we’ll be exploring some of these ideas in AI to understand where they came from and what problems they solved.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "487cdf57-5012-4fe9-a475-0b0fb844b624",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e7f0096-f270-421a-bdba-088c9ca33cfe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb332a33-8790-4c60-8115-0813ff43c4d9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb332a33-8790-4c60-8115-0813ff43c4d9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6e7f0096-f270-421a-bdba-088c9ca33cfe"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7b263f9-494d-4ece-8fb4-0e188f4dd375",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students explore different disciplines and technologies in AI’s history. The goal is to help students see that AI has come from work and research across many areas, to give students context for key milestones in AI’s development, and to give students the chance to consider how those milestones impacted today’s technology.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Pioneers and Ideas"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7b263f9-494d-4ece-8fb4-0e188f4dd375",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "535d7ff4-79eb-479c-b5fa-a9a6f2be43d2",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI isn’t just one idea or algorithm: it’s the result of many years of development and problem solving. The innovations that made AI possible came from research and ideas in multiple different fields.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "535d7ff4-79eb-479c-b5fa-a9a6f2be43d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e5f739e6-4818-4ace-a6b8-af6c6cde9024",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *interdisciplinary* and describe AI as the result of interdisciplinary research.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show some of the fields that have contributed to research in AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI Innovations Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e5f739e6-4818-4ace-a6b8-af6c6cde9024",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "434527ec-5b6c-40e1-9b3c-e49c0b7c29bb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "There are a lot of milestones in the history of AI. In this activity, you should pick one innovation related to AI that you’re excited about and learn more about it.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "434527ec-5b6c-40e1-9b3c-e49c0b7c29bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d711d8fb-8a96-48ad-aed9-f755bc1990d0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students use AI Chat to explore different disciplines that have contributed to AI’s development and complete the “Exploring AI Milestones” page of their Activity Guide based on their interests.\n- Level 2: Students complete the formative assessment.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through Level 1, help students find a discipline and technology they’re interested in. Each discipline could lead to multiple technologies that students could explore: remind students that they don’t need to know about all of the technologies yet, they only need to pick one that looks interesting to them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d711d8fb-8a96-48ad-aed9-f755bc1990d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92b30b86-0a39-44a9-b7f6-16433e7e4fe6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Contributions to AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- Level 1 is a choice level where students can choose a discipline of interest to them. If students finish early, they can explore another discipline to get a different perspective on other innovations that contributed to the development of AI. Or if students are excited about a different discipline or technology not covered in the level, you can encourage them to do their own independent research to complete the activity.\n- If time permits and if you have space in your classroom, students can draw or write about their chosen innovation in AI on a post-it note or on a sheet of paper and add it to an “AI Timeline” poster that can be hung in the classroom as a visual representation of the progression of AI."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Review student responses to the formative assessment to check that students can identify one or more ways that AI has developed in its history."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92b30b86-0a39-44a9-b7f6-16433e7e4fe6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab14d672-c620-4b6f-bec0-bbf1a208fe90",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students consider predictions for the future of AI and make their own predictions. The goal is to help students see that today’s technology is not the end state for AI: technology continues to improve and develop, and students are encouraged to imagine what that might look like.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Future of AI"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab14d672-c620-4b6f-bec0-bbf1a208fe90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e67f4586-3310-4a54-b9b5-9b4269fd0007",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI is still being developed, and it might look very different a few years from now than it does today.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e67f4586-3310-4a54-b9b5-9b4269fd0007",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74d65fc9-5147-4ef2-910f-eea42156d725",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students find a partner and direct students to the “Predict the Future” page of their Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74d65fc9-5147-4ef2-910f-eea42156d725",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9c5aef15-bcf5-44ba-ac7a-92e7f4da43b4",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Everyone has different ideas for what AI might look like in the future. Some of those predictions might come true soon, some might take a long time, and others might not happen at all.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9c5aef15-bcf5-44ba-ac7a-92e7f4da43b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6cd0c03-92ba-4a51-8b70-d7cca13f327d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students complete the “Predict the Future” page of their Activity Guide.\n- Students will first read a set of predictions about the future of AI. In pairs, they should discuss what predictions they think are realistic or unrealistic. They should also talk about what predictions they are excited by.\n- Then, students make their own prediction for what they want to see in the future of AI.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As pairs fill in the activity guide, check on how students are thinking about the future and how they imagine it to be. When students are making their own prediction, this may be where students are most likely to provide vague answers like “scientists need to figure it out” or “we need better computers.” Help them get specific by drawing on the first activity: each step in AI’s history came from someone solving a particular problem in a particular discipline. What discipline could contribute to this innovation? What questions might people in that discipline need to ask?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "The questions in the “Predict the Future” section of the Activity Guide can be used to check student understanding of the lesson objectives. Check for whether students articulate their vision for a future of AI and what technical or societal advancements would make it possible."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6cd0c03-92ba-4a51-8b70-d7cca13f327d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a78f88ad-fa92-4421-9e89-616cdbe82d1b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "879e0782-61b8-47ca-8107-4c0d72d0dd3e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students take out their Logic-Based AI Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** In their Unit Guide, students answer the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "879e0782-61b8-47ca-8107-4c0d72d0dd3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "42e15a05-46ba-4120-acb6-06452cf97fc9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "92e390b2-3a12-4e48-abe0-1336d18d1354",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students learn about the task of sentiment analysis by playing a mini-game as a class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce What’s the Vibe?",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "What's the Vibe?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "92e390b2-3a12-4e48-abe0-1336d18d1354",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "558c6b08-46ca-446d-bee9-38256e45eb46",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "“Let’s play a game called ‘What’s the Vibe?’! Here’s how the game works: I’ll show a sentence on the screen, and you’ll need to shout out, as quickly as you can, how you think the person who said the sentence is feeling. Ready?”",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "558c6b08-46ca-446d-bee9-38256e45eb46",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c0f0a87a-87b2-4b4f-9b9e-66be0d4e3e68",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a sentence. Let students, all together, shout out how they think the person who said this sentence is feeling. Encourage quick, gut reactions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** As students shout out their guesses, repeat a few of their suggestions back to the class."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c0f0a87a-87b2-4b4f-9b9e-66be0d4e3e68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7c4e411-fa53-4721-8f18-bd4b4572c341",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Continue showing sentences and listening to students shout out their guesses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did you know how the person who said each sentence might be feeling?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt students to reflect on the idea of sentiment analysis before defining it formally. Emphasize how quickly students could tell how the person who said each sentence might be feeling: what knowledge did they rely on to arrive at that conclusion so quickly? Good responses might name specific skills: such as knowledge of emotionally charged vocabulary (e.g., “proud,” “awful,” “amazing”), or previous lived experience (e.g., “I’ve been to a disappointing movie before”). If students offer generic responses, such as “We just know!” or “The person told us how they feel!”, help them zero in on the specific skills or experiences they might have relied on. Once you’ve gathered ideas from 3–4 students, continue on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7c4e411-fa53-4721-8f18-bd4b4572c341",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72c035e4-1b00-469c-81d3-0821f321c6cb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "“In the world of artificial intelligence, there’s actually a word we can use to describe the challenge of figuring out how someone might be feeling based on the words they say. Let’s take a look.”",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72c035e4-1b00-469c-81d3-0821f321c6cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3518131d-a548-43a9-80e1-be47e9c30bfe",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define sentiment analysis."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3518131d-a548-43a9-80e1-be47e9c30bfe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "086a7f26-7cae-4062-b3da-e074541531f7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"Just like you were doing a moment ago, sentiment analysis is about figuring out how people feel from text alone. It’s a common problem AI is being used to solve. Sentiment analysis can help businesses uses product reviews to guess how customers feel, help researchers uses social media to understand public reactions to major events, and even help doctors use patient notes to sense how patients are feeling—among many other uses!\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "086a7f26-7cae-4062-b3da-e074541531f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dee0f895-5826-4ba6-a076-bdbee50124c3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"Today, we’ll explore how we can tell computers how to solve problems like sentiment analysis.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dee0f895-5826-4ba6-a076-bdbee50124c3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad38f9e4-c597-4481-8b14-a095ab7332be",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"To tell a computer how to accomplish a task like sentiment analysis, we’ll create an algorithm.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad38f9e4-c597-4481-8b14-a095ab7332be",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b82aacc6-86e1-4e44-9c5b-49d203973454",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define algorithm.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be familiar with the word “algorithm” through their varied of prior experiences. Some students may have already taken a computer science course in which algorithms were defined. Other students may have heard it used in popular culture, as a reference to “the algorithm” that decides what they see online. Prompt a few students to reflect on where they’ve heard this word before and what it means to them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b82aacc6-86e1-4e44-9c5b-49d203973454",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "261adbd1-f1ab-4f11-b592-b84fe1d65beb",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "261adbd1-f1ab-4f11-b592-b84fe1d65beb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "08ea39fc-1f64-460a-ae27-40d260588810"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7599627e-6cd5-43f2-abc1-ae3bdf41b85f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Get students started with considering how a computer could perform sentiment analysis. In their Activity Guide, they’ll come up with a general strategy a computer might be able to use. Later, they’ll turn it into pseudocode.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce students to Career Journeys: connections between lesson content and careers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the career students will explore in this lesson.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Exploring Sentiment Analysis"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7599627e-6cd5-43f2-abc1-ae3bdf41b85f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b7a41e5-73a5-4b1c-9a7a-a7e03fe22fe9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"Today we’ll explore the career of a market analyst: someone who uses data to help companies understand what people want and need. Market analysts use sentiment analysis regularly: it helps them understand how people are feeling about a topic.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b7a41e5-73a5-4b1c-9a7a-a7e03fe22fe9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dbbd6362-cab1-4aa5-acd5-8ffc9855239b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Exploring Sentiment Analysis activity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define strategy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define pseudocode.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Describe an example problem to solve.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share an example strategy for solving the problem.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Share example pseudocode that implements the strategy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Prepare students to start the Sentiment Analysis Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student pair a copy of the Sentiment Analysis Activity Guide."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dbbd6362-cab1-4aa5-acd5-8ffc9855239b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b84cbe1-a690-4f19-8d43-ecdaa8fdd215",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"Your challenge is this: given just the word lists on your Activity Guide, how could you tell whether a sentence like 'I had a wonderful day' is happy or sad? Don't worry about being a computer just yet—focus on brainstorming a strategy a human could follow step by step.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b84cbe1-a690-4f19-8d43-ecdaa8fdd215",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a9b91c71-56e8-418f-a011-68877bd30a05",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While pairs brainstorm, look for groups that:\n- Are stuck on where to start. Prompt them with: \"What if you went through the sentence one word at a time? What would you check for each word?\"\n- Have already landed on a strategy. Push for depth. You could ask: \"What does your strategy do if a sentence has the same number of happy and sad words?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What strategy did you come up with? What are its strengths and weaknesses?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Prompt pairs to share their strategies and learn from each other. Try to surface differences in approaches (e.g., counting happy vs. sad words, handling ties) so that students see there’s no single right answer. Look out for and validate observations about the limits of the word-list approach (e.g., that it may struggle with negation or sarcasm). End the discussion by pointing out the limitations students are finding are real limitations that motivate the AI techniques students will study later in the course."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a9b91c71-56e8-418f-a011-68877bd30a05",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "870006fe-8f6a-4ca4-8ec2-78006651899c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"We've thought about some interesting strategies. But to have a computer run these strategies, we need to write them down precisely. That's what we'll do next.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "870006fe-8f6a-4ca4-8ec2-78006651899c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc611dd6-9b49-417a-8ec4-b5baa672d0f9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students individually translate their brainstormed strategy into pseudocode, test it by tracing it on sample sentences, and then refine it with help from their partner. By the end, they have a written, tested, and revised algorithm they can explain and justify.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Writing Pseudocode"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc611dd6-9b49-417a-8ec4-b5baa672d0f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d230c7a-35dd-4dcd-a4a6-ba6cc5107bf3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"Turn to the next page of your Activity Guide and try writing your strategy as pseudocode. When you're done, trace through it on the sample sentences: that is, follow your own instructions one step at a time and see if it gives you the right answer.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d230c7a-35dd-4dcd-a4a6-ba6cc5107bf3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40864c6c-8fd3-420c-89ba-794364109ece",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the next page of the Sentiment Analysis activity guide. Have each student individually write their pseudocode and then trace it on the sample sentences before moving on.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** While students write and trace their pseudocode, watch for:\n- Students skipping steps that \"seem obvious.\" Encourage explicitness: \"How would someone reading this know to do X?\"\n- Students stuck on syntax. Remind them that pseudocode doesn't have to look like any specific language—clarity matters more than style.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to swap their Activity Guide with their partner, so that each partner can follow the other’s pseudocode.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to pay attention to how their partner follows their pseudocode, and to make any revisions based on seeing their partner do so."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40864c6c-8fd3-420c-89ba-794364109ece",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8b986a17-9b56-425b-a44f-c2c58eccdd2c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this Activity:** Students take a final pass at refining their algorithm, with the help of AI.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Refining Algorithms"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8b986a17-9b56-425b-a44f-c2c58eccdd2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37a034a7-e979-4d7c-8a98-b4c952e6dd2d",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\"Now, let’s work individually to assess and refine your algorithm with the help of AI. You're in charge throughout—your job is to evaluate each suggestion the AI makes, rather than accept it automatically.\"",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37a034a7-e979-4d7c-8a98-b4c952e6dd2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f8e0ac5-ab69-4753-891f-fa3490a91690",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Refining Your Algorithm"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f8e0ac5-ab69-4753-891f-fa3490a91690",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c25abb8-be54-4c70-a1c1-12097ee42662",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Levels 1–2.\n- Level 1: Students individually chat with AI to refine their algorithm.\n- Level 2: Students individually document their refined algorithm, describing what they changed and why.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work through the levels, watch for students accepting AI suggestions without questioning them. Push them to think: \"Why do you think the AI suggested that change? Does it actually improve your algorithm, or just make it more complicated? What new case did using AI surface that you hadn't considered?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 to gauge whether students can engage critically with refining an algorithm. Look for students providing sufficient reasoning for incorporating or rejecting suggestions from the AI Chat level."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c25abb8-be54-4c70-a1c1-12097ee42662",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8356e64d-8eec-4e9e-b58a-cfedf30f4366"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bd6ab62-2f90-445e-ae96-ba6f0f283162",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to answer the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bd6ab62-2f90-445e-ae96-ba6f0f283162",
        "lesson_activity.key": "17217f37-52f9-491a-910e-a55fd36628ad"
      }
    }
  ],
